WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.180
Defensive C sharp programming is a set of repeatable habits.

2
00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:07.560
Use types and nullability to make assumptions visible.

3
00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:09.500
Validate at boundaries.

4
00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:11.760
Handle expected failures deliberately.

5
00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:13.620
Keep resource use bounded.

6
00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:15.000
Protect secrets.

7
00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:17.100
Keep shared state reviewable.

8
00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:21.040
Log enough to operate the system safely without exposing sensitive data.

9
00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:25.080
The modules in this course connect because production systems connect.

10
00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:27.660
Type design affects validation.

11
00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:30.080
Validation affects error handling.

12
00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:32.100
Error handling affects logs.

13
00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:33.880
Logs affect operations.

14
00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:36.140
Dependencies affect deployment.

15
00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:40.240
Deployment affects how safely the code behaves in the real environment.

16
00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:43.640
Small habits reduce recurring risk.

17
00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:55.260
A clear constructor, a bounded read, a cancellation token, a parameterized query, a redacted log field, a reviewed package update, or a safer return type may not feel dramatic.

18
00:00:55.360 --> 00:01:00.720
Together, those habits make the codebase easier to trust and easier to change.

19
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:04.280
Teams do not need to wait for a major rewrite to improve.

20
00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:16.620
They can add nullable checks, reduce loose primitives, review validation boundaries, improve error handling, clean up secrets, tighten logging, and make dependency review more consistent over time.

21
00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:21.380
The closing idea is simple: defensive programming is responsible engineering.

22
00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:34.040
It protects users, operators, future maintainers, and the business by making software behavior clearer when conditions are imperfect, which is exactly when good engineering matters most.
